{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"fetch","tool":"fetch","arguments":{"max_length":8000,"start_index":5000,"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anti-corruption_campaign_under_Xi_Jinping"},"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Contents of https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anti-corruption_campaign_under_Xi_Jinping:\nHe_Weidong \"He Weidong\") and [Zhang Youxia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zhang_Youxia \"Zhang Youxia\") in 2025 and 2026 respectively. and former [Xinjiang CCP committee secretary](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Party_Secretary_of_Xinjiang \"Party Secretary of Xinjiang\") [Ma Xingrui](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ma_Xingrui \"Ma Xingrui\") in 2026. As of 2023, approximately 2.3 million government officials had been [prosecuted](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prosecuted \"Prosecuted\"). The campaign formed a central component of a broader initiative aimed at curbing corruption within the CCP and reinforcing internal unity through a process termed [self-revolution](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Self-revolution \"Self-revolution\"). It has since become one of the defining features of Xi's political legacy and has been cited as a key factor that allowed Xi to consolidate power.\n\n[![](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Wang_Qishan_in_2016.jpg/120px-Wang_Qishan_in_2016.jpg)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Wang_Qishan_in_2016.jpg)\n\n[![](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Zhao_Leji_meets_Keir_Starmer_Jan_2026.jpg/120px-Zhao_Leji_meets_Keir_Starmer_Jan_2026.jpg)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Zhao_Leji_meets_Keir_Starmer_Jan_2026.jpg)\n\n[![](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Li_Xi_%28cropped%29_%282025%29_01.jpg/120px-Li_Xi_%28cropped%29_%282025%29_01.jpg)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Li_Xi_(cropped)_(2025)_01.jpg)\n\n\n\nThe agency directly charged with overseeing the campaign is the CCDI, which, at the time of the campaign, was headed by [Secretary](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Secretary_of_the_Central_Commission_for_Discipline_Inspection \"Secretary of the Central Commission for Discipline Inspection\") [Wang Qishan](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wang_Qishan \"Wang Qishan\"), a politician known for his work in the financial sector and one of the seven members of the CCP Politburo Standing Committee.[[1]](#cite_note-1) Wang was in charge of the day-to-day execution of the campaign. The CCDI's official mandate is to enforce party discipline, combat malfeasance, and punish party members for committing offenses. The CCDI is an internal agency of the party and therefore does not have judicial authority. In general, the CCDI investigates officials and, when necessary, forwards evidence gathered to judicial organs, such as the [Supreme People's Procuratorate](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Supreme_People's_Procuratorate \"Supreme People's Procuratorate\") (in charge of investigation and prosecution), which proceeds to charge the accused with criminal wrongdoing and move the case to trial.[[2]](#cite_note-2)\n\nWhile the CCDI formally reports into the CCP National Congress, nominally the highest representative body of the party which gathers once every five years, and is intended to be an 'independent' agency from a constitutional standpoint, in practice ultimate oversight of the agency falls under the purview of Xi Jinping by virtue of holding the office of [General Secretary](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/General_Secretary_of_the_Chinese_Communist_Party \"General Secretary of the Chinese Communist Party\") (i.e., *de facto* [leader](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paramount_leader \"Paramount leader\")).[[3]](#cite_note-3) Xi, who also directs anti-graft efforts of the military through his holding the office of [Chairman of the Central Military Commission](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chairman_of_the_Central_Military_Commission_(China) \"Chairman of the Central Military Commission (China)\") (i.e., [commander-in-chief](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Supreme_Military_Command_of_the_People's_Republic_of_China \"Supreme Military Command of the People's Republic of China\")). The majority of reporting on the campaign by media sources has highlighted Xi Jinping's direct involvement in managing the campaign, which has become a central hallmark of his term in office. However, formal disciplinary measures meted out to high-ranking officials, such as former Politburo members, must undergo ratification by the sitting Politburo.[[4]](#cite_note-4) According to *The Wall Street Journal*, anti-corruption punishment to officials at or above the vice ministerial level need approval from Xi.[[5]](#cite_note-Chin-2021-5)\n\nThe power of anti-corruption is centralized in the CCP Politburo Standing Committee by undermining the original functions of the local Discipline Inspection Commissions. Coordination of [anti-corruption](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anti-corruption \"Anti-corruption\") efforts in the provinces and state-owned enterprises has been carried out by \"central inspection teams\" (中央巡视组), which reports to the [Central Leading Group for Inspection Work](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Central_Leading_Group_for_Inspection_Work \"Central Leading Group for Inspection Work\"), which, like the CCDI, was also led by Wang Qishan. The inspection teams are typically 'stationed' for a few months at the organization they were tasked with overseeing, and are in charge of thorough audits into the conduct of officials and organizational practices. The inspection teams send the results of the audits to the CCDI to enact formal investigative procedures such as *[Shuanggui](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shuanggui \"Shuanggui\")* (the practice of detaining individual party members for investigation).[[6]](#cite_note-6)[*[better source needed](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Verifiability#Questionable_sources \"Wikipedia:Verifiability\")*]\n\nThe proposed constitutional changes published on 25 February 2018 envisioned the creation of a new anti-graft state agency that merges various anti-corruption government departments.[[7]](#cite_note-7) The thus formed [National Supervisory Commission](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Supervisory_Commission \"National Supervisory Commission\") is the highest state supervisory body in the country, and holds same constitutional status as that of the [State Council](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/State_Council_of_China \"State Council of China\"), of the [Supreme People's Court](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Supreme_People's_Court \"Supreme People's Court\"), and of the [Supreme People's Procuratorate](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Supreme_People's_Procuratorate \"Supreme People's Procuratorate\"), outranking courts and the office of the prosecutor.[[8]](#cite_note-:Tsang&Cheung2-8): 57–58 Effectively functioning as the state arm of the CCDI, the commission covers all public servants, whether or not they're CCP members. Additionally under Xi, the resident discipline offices and inspectors have been embedded to cover all central agencies and state ministries.[[9]](#cite_note-:6-9)\n\n[![](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Distribution_map_of_implicated_officials_%28Provincial-Ministerial_level_and_higher%29_since_18th_CPC_National_Congress.jpg/330px-Distribution_map_of_implicated_officials_%28Provincial-Ministerial_level_and_higher%29_since_18th_CPC_National_Congress.jpg)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Distribution_map_of_implicated_officials_(Provincial-Ministerial_level_and_higher)_since_18th_CPC_National_Congress.jpg)\n\nA distribution map of implicated officials (Provincial-Ministerial level and higher) since the 18th National Congress of the Chinese Communist Party.\n\nAnti-corruption efforts have been on the agenda of successive Chinese leaders, though the effectiveness of these campaigns has varied. Since the [reform and opening up](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reform_and_opening_up \"Reform and opening up\") began in 1978, political corruption in China has grown significantly. The types of offenses vary, though usually they involve trading bribes for political favours, such as local businesses trying to secure large government contracts or subordinates seeking promotions for higher office.[[10]](#cite_note-10)[[11]](#cite_note-11)\n\n> We should continue to catch **\"tigers\", as well as \"flies\"** when dea\n\n<error>Content truncated. Call the fetch tool with a start_index of 13000 to get more content.</error>"}],"structured_content":null},"error":null,"status":"completed"}}
