{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_37","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"fetch","tool":"fetch","arguments":{"max_length":20000,"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foreign_policy_of_Xi_Jinping"},"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Contents of https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foreign_policy_of_Xi_Jinping:\nThe **foreign policy of Xi Jinping** concerns the policies of the [People's Republic of China](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China \"China\")'s [Xi Jinping](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xi_Jinping \"Xi Jinping\") with respect to other nations. Xi became the [General Secretary of the Chinese Communist Party](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/General_Secretary_of_the_Chinese_Communist_Party \"General Secretary of the Chinese Communist Party\") in 2012 and became the [President of China](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/President_of_China \"President of China\") in 2013.\n\nXi has reportedly taken a hard-line on security issues as well as foreign affairs, projecting a more nationalistic and assertive China on the world stage.[[1]](#cite_note-kuhn2-1) His political program calls for a China more united and [confident of its own value system and political structure](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Confidence_doctrine \"Confidence doctrine\").[[2]](#cite_note-scmp32-2) Xi Jinping's \"[major-country diplomacy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Major-country_diplomacy \"Major-country diplomacy\")\" doctrine has replaced the earlier Deng Xiaoping era slogan of \"[keep a low profile](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taoguang_Yanghui \"Taoguang Yanghui\")\" and has legitimized a more active role for China on the world stage, particularly with regards to reform of the international order, engaging in open ideological competition with the West, and assuming a greater responsibility for global affairs in accordance with China's rising power and status.[[3]](#cite_note-3) Xi has advocated for diplomats to adopt a more assertive style, commonly expressed as [wolf warrior diplomacy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wolf_warrior_diplomacy \"Wolf warrior diplomacy\").\n\nIn setting foreign policy, Xi favors an approach of baseline thinking, in which China explicitly states red line that other countries must not cross. In the Chinese perspective, taking tough positions on these matters reduces strategic uncertainty.\n\nXi takes a strong personal interest in foreign affairs.[[4]](#cite_note-:42-4): 14 In his first five years in office, Xi flew over 350,000 miles, visited five continents, and gave over one hundred speeches to foreign audiences.[[4]](#cite_note-:42-4): 14–15 In doing so, he became the first Chinese leader to outpace his American presidential counterparts in foreign travel.[[4]](#cite_note-:42-4): 15 Xi's extensive schedule of phone and video foreign meetings as part of his \"cloud diplomacy\" (云外交) received prominent attention in Chinese media, similar to in-person foreign visits.[[4]](#cite_note-:42-4): 15\n\nXi has overseen a shift towards a [Chinese foreign policy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foreign_policy_of_China \"Foreign policy of China\") which, as contrasted with [the approaches of Chinese leaders since Deng Xiaoping](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_foreign_relations_of_China \"History of foreign relations of China\"), is more assertive in acting proactively rather than reacting, and more willing to forcefully assert national interests rather than compromise them.[[4]](#cite_note-:42-4): 78 This transition was marked by the October 2013 announcement of \"striving for achievement\".[[5]](#cite_note-:13-5): 22 According to Xi, the \"primary theme of China's foreign policy should be the striving for achievements, moving forward along with time changes, and acting more proactively.\"[[4]](#cite_note-:42-4): 85 Xi calls on diplomats to demonstrate a fighting spirit, which has been expressed in the form of wolf warrior diplomacy.[[4]](#cite_note-:42-4): 79\n\n[![](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/China-Central_Asia_Summit_20230519_02.jpg/250px-China-Central_Asia_Summit_20230519_02.jpg)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:China-Central_Asia_Summit_20230519_02.jpg)\n\nXi Jinping at the [China-Central Asia Summit](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China-Central_Asia_Summit \"China-Central Asia Summit\") on 19 May 2023\n\nDuring the [Xi Jinping era](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China_under_Xi_Jinping \"China under Xi Jinping\"), the [Community of Common Destiny](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Community_of_Common_Destiny \"Community of Common Destiny\") has become China's most important foreign relations formulation.[[6]](#cite_note-:2-6): 6\n\nXi advocates \"baseline thinking\" in China's foreign policy: setting explicit red lines that other countries must not cross.[[4]](#cite_note-:42-4): 86 In the Chinese perspective, these tough stances on baseline issues reduce strategic uncertainty, preventing other nations from misjudging China's positions or underestimating China's resolve in asserting what it perceives to be in its national interest.[[4]](#cite_note-:42-4): 86\n\nBefore 2017, Xi stated that China should participate in forming a new global order.[[4]](#cite_note-:42-4): 240 This position changed in 2017, when Xi articulated the \"Two Guidances\": (1) China should guide the global community in building a more just and reasonable world order, and (2) that China should guide the global community in safeguarding international security.[[4]](#cite_note-:42-4): 240\n\nDuring the [COVID-19 pandemic](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/COVID-19_pandemic \"COVID-19 pandemic\"), China engaged in [mask diplomacy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mask_diplomacy \"Mask diplomacy\"), a practice facilitated by its early success in responding to the pandemic.[[4]](#cite_note-:42-4): 90 Chinese ownership of much of the global medical supply chain enhanced its ability to send doctors and medical equipment to suffering countries.[[4]](#cite_note-:42-4): 90 China soon followed its mask diplomacy with [vaccine diplomacy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vaccine_diplomacy \"Vaccine diplomacy\").[[4]](#cite_note-:42-4): 90 China's infection rates were sufficiently low that it could send vaccines abroad without domestic objections.[[4]](#cite_note-:42-4): 90 Academic [Suisheng Zhao](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Suisheng_Zhao \"Suisheng Zhao\") writes that \"[j]ust by showing up and helping plug the colossal gaps in the global supply, China gained ground.\"[[4]](#cite_note-:42-4): 90\n\n[![](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/16th_BRICS_Summit_family_photograph_%282024%29.jpg/250px-16th_BRICS_Summit_family_photograph_%282024%29.jpg)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:16th_BRICS_Summit_family_photograph_(2024).jpg)\n\n[BRICS](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BRICS \"BRICS\") representatives at the [16th BRICS summit](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/16th_BRICS_summit \"16th BRICS summit\") in [Kazan](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kazan \"Kazan\"), Russia, 23 October 2024\n\nIn the Xi era, China takes the position that unilateral economic restrictions and trade discrimination are impermissible measures for countries to use in achieving foreign policy goals and has positioned itself generally as a proponent of global free trade.[[7]](#cite_note-:5-7): 16 In his January 2021 speech during the [Davos Economic Forum](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Davos_Forums \"Davos Forums\"), Xi called for abandonment of deliberate decoupling and the use of sanctions, stating that maintaining a commitment to diplomacy and a multilateral trade regime were important.[[7]](#cite_note-:5-7): 16–17\n\nDuring the Xi Jinping administration, China seeks to shape international norms and rules in emerging policy areas where China has an advantage as an early participant.[[8]](#cite_note-:Tsang&Cheung-8): 188 Xi describes such areas as \"new frontiers,\" and they include policy areas such as space, deep sea, polar regions, the Internet, nuclear safety, anticorruption, and climate change.[[8]](#cite_note-:Tsang&Cheung-8): 188\n\nIn foreign policy announcements, Xi sometimes refers to \"[Great changes unseen in a century](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_changes_unseen_in_a_century \"Great changes unseen in a century\")\".[[9]](#cite_note-:4-9): 34 The phrase refers to the perceived [decline of United States power](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_decline \"American decline\") both domestically and internationally, as well as the broader fragmentation of Western powers.[[9]](#cite_note-:4-9): 34 Xi also cites foreign friends of China in his foreign policy discourse to acknowledge other countries' sacrifices to assist in China's national liberation, particularly with regard to the [Second Sino-Japanese war](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_Sino-Japanese_War \"Second Sino-Japanese War\").[[10]](#cite_note-:Shan-10): 42 For example, during diplomatic visits to other countries, Xi has praised the contributions of \"friends\" such as [Claire Lee Chennault](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Claire_Lee_Chennault \"Claire Lee Chennault\"), [Norman Bethune](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norman_Bethune \"Norman Bethune\"), [Dawarkanath Kotnis](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dwarkanath_Kotnis \"Dwarkanath Kotnis\"), and Soviet pilots.[[10]](#cite_note-:Shan-10): 42\n\n## Republic of China (Taiwan)\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Foreign_policy_of_Xi_Jinping&action=edit&section=2 \"Edit section: Republic of China (Taiwan)\")]\n\n[![](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/2015_Ma%E2%80%93Xi_Meeting_08.jpg/250px-2015_Ma%E2%80%93Xi_Meeting_08.jpg)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:2015_Ma%E2%80%93Xi_Meeting_08.jpg)\n\nXi Jinping met with then-Taiwanese president [Ma Ying-jeou](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ma_Ying-jeou \"Ma Ying-jeou\") in November 2015 in their capacity as the leader of mainland China and Taiwan respectively.\n\nIn 2015, Xi [met](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_Ma%E2%80%93Xi_meeting \"First Ma–Xi meeting\") with [Ma Ying-jeou](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ma_Ying-jeou \"Ma Ying-jeou\"), the [president of the Republic of China](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/President_of_the_Republic_of_China \"President of the Republic of China\") (ROC, Taiwan) which marked the first time the political leaders of both sides of the [Taiwan Strait](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taiwan_Strait \"Taiwan Strait\") met since major hostilities of the [Chinese Civil War](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_Civil_War \"Chinese Civil War\") ceased in 1951.[[11]](#cite_note-11) Xi said that China and Taiwan are \"[one family](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zhonghua_minzu \"Zhonghua minzu\")\" that cannot be pulled apart.[[12]](#cite_note-12) However, the relations started deteriorating after [Tsai Ing-wen](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tsai_Ing-wen \"Tsai Ing-wen\") of the [Democratic Progressive Party](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Democratic_Progressive_Party \"Democratic Progressive Party\") (DPP) won the [presidential elections in 2016](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2016_Taiwanese_presidential_election \"2016 Taiwanese presidential election\").[[13]](#cite_note-13)\n\nOn 12 March 2016, Xi stated that the [1992 Consensus](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1992_Consensus \"1992 Consensus\") was \"the greatest common denominator and political bottom line for the peaceful development of cross-strait relations\".[[14]](#cite_note-:12-14): 13\n\nIn the [19th National Congress of the Chinese Communist Party](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/19th_National_Congress_of_the_Chinese_Communist_Party \"19th National Congress of the Chinese Communist Party\") held in 2017, Xi reaffirmed six of the nine principles that had been affirmed continuously since the CCP's [16th National Congress](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/16th_National_Congress_of_the_Chinese_Communist_Party \"16th National Congress of the Chinese Communist Party\") in 2002, with the notable exception of \"Placing hopes on the [Taiwan people](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/People_of_Taiwan \"People of Taiwan\") as a force to help bring about [unification](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_unification \"Chinese unification\").\"[[15]](#cite_note-Brookings_R.C._Bush2-15) According to the [Brookings Institution](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brookings_Institution \"Brookings Institution\"), Xi used stronger language on potential [Taiwan independence](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taiwan_independence_movement \"Taiwan independence movement\") than his predecessors towards previous DPP governments in Taiwan.[[15]](#cite_note-Brookings_R.C._Bush2-15) He said that \"we will never allow any person, any organisation, or any political party to split any part of the Chinese territory from China at any time at any form.\"[[15]](#cite_note-Brookings_R.C._Bush2-15) Xi stated that people from Taiwan could receive national treatment in pursuing careers on the mainland and in February 2018 the PRC government announced 31 preferential policies for Taiwan people on matters of industry, finance, taxation, land use, employment, education, and health care.[[14]](#cite_note-:12-14): 13\n\nIn March 2018, Xi said that Taiwan would face the \"punishment of history\" for any attempts at separatism.[[16]](#cite_note-Wen-2018-16)\n\nIn a January 2019 speech commemorating the 40th anniversary of the \"Message to Compatriots in Taiwan,\" Xi Jinping called for \"peaceful reunification with Taiwan\" in accordance with the \"One China principle\" and the 1992 Consensus.[[17]](#cite_note-:Chen-17): 240 In Xi's view, the Taiwan issue emerged from China's weakness dating back to the Opium Wars and after World War II, the \"two sides of the Taiwan straits fell into a special state of protracted political confrontation due to the civil war in China and the interferences of foreign forces.\"[[17]](#cite_note-:Chen-17): 240–241 Xi Jinping called on Taiwan to reject formal independence from China, saying: \"We make no promise to renounce the use of force and reserve the option of taking all necessary means.\" Those options, he said, could be used against \"external interference.\" Xi also said that they \"are willing to create broad space for peaceful reunification, but will leave no room for any form of separatist activities.\"[[18]](#cite_note-18)[[19]](#cite_note-19) President Tsai responded to the speech by saying Taiwan would not accept a [one country, two systems](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/One_country,_two_systems \"One country, two systems\") arrangement with the mainland, while stressing the need for all cross-strait negotiations to be on a government-to-government basis.[[20]](#cite_note-20)\n\nXi states that unification with Taiwan should occur peacefully because that is \"most in line with the overall interest of the Chinese nation, including Taiwan compatriots.\"[[21]](#cite_note-:0-21) In a speech at the [100th Anniversary of the Chinese Communist Party](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/100th_Anniversary_of_the_Chinese_Communist_Party \"100th Anniversary of the Chinese Communist Party\"), Xi stated:[[22]](#cite_note-:1-22)\n\n> Resolving the Taiwan question and realizing China’s complete reunification is a historic mission and an unshakable commitment of the Communist Party of China. It is also a shared aspiration of all the sons and daughters of the Chinese nation. We will uphold the one-China principle and the [1992 Consensus](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1992_Consensus \"1992 Consensus\"), and advance peaceful national reunification. All of us, compatriots on both sides of the Taiwan Strait, must come together and move forward in unison. We must take resolute action to utterly defeat any attempt toward “Taiwan independence,” and work together to create a bright future for national rejuvenation. No one should underestimate the resolve, the will, and the ability of the Chinese people to defend their national sovereignty and territorial integrity.\n\nXi has also said that unification under a \"one country, two systems\" approach would be appropriate.[[21]](#cite_note-:0-21) The American think tank [Council on Foreign Relations](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Council_on_Foreign_Relations \"Council on Foreign Relations\") described Xi's position on Taiwan as continuing consistent with the PRC's 1979 shift from \"liberation\" of Taiwan to \"peaceful unification\" with Taiwan.[[22]](#cite_note-:1-22)\n\nIn 2022, after the PRC military exercises around Taiwan, the PRC published a white paper called \"[The Taiwan Question and China's Reunification in the New Era](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Taiwan_Question_and_China's_Reunification_in_the_New_Era \"The Taiwan Question and China's Reunification in the New Era\"),\" which was the first white paper regards to Taiwan since 2000.[[23]](#cite_note-Millson-2022-23) The paper urged Taiwan to become a [special administrative region](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special_administrative_regions_of_China \"Special administrative regions of China\") of the PRC under the one country two systems formula,[[23]](#cite_note-Millson-2022-23) and said that \"a small number of countries, the U.S. foremost amongst them\" are \"using Taiwan to contain China.\"[[24]](#cite_note-Falconer-2022-24) Notably, the new white paper excluded a part that previously said the PRC would not send troops or officials to Taiwan after unification.[[24]](#cite_note-Falconer-2022-24)\n\nDuring Xi's administration, China has maintained cordial relationships with each Africa government except [Eswatini](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eswatini \"Eswatini\"), which recognizes Taiwan but not the PRC.[[6]](#cite_note-:2-6) Xi's diplomatic rhetoric links the China-Africa Community of Shared Future to the concept of the [Chinese Dream](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_Dream \"Chinese Dream\").[[6]](#cite_note-:2-6): 21 Although Xi has generally prioritized relations between the CCP and political parties in the [Global South](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_South \"Global South\"), Xi has especially prioritized such party-to-party relations in Africa.[[6]](#cite_note-:2-6): 85\n\nAt the 2018 [Forum on China-Africa Cooperation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forum_on_China%E2%80%93Africa_Cooperation \"Forum on China–Africa Cooperation\"), Xi emphasized the \"Five Nos\" which guide its foreign policy in dealing with African countries and other developing countries: (1) non-interference in other countries' pursuit of development paths suitable to their national conditions, (2) non-interference in domestic affairs, (3) not imposing China's will on others, (4) not attaching political conditions to foreign aid, and (5) not seeking political self-interest in investment and financing.[[25]](#cite_note-:05-25): 108–109\n\nUnder Xi, China has cut back lending to Africa after fears that African countries couldn't repay their debts to China.[[26]](#cite_note-26) Xi has also promised that China would write off debts of some African countries.[[27]](#cite_note-27) In November 2021, Xi promised African nations 1 billion doses of China's COVID-19 vaccines, which was in addition to the 200 million already supplied before. This has been said to be part of China's vaccine diplomacy.[[28]](#cite_note-28)\n\nIn January 2026, Canadian Prime Minister [Mark Carney](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mark_Carney \"Mark Carney\") made an official visit to China and met with Xi Jinping.[[29]](#cite_note-29) During his official visit to Beijing, Carney announced the formation of a \"new strategic partnership\" with China.[[30]](#cite_note-30)\n\n[![](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Xi_Jinping_in_USA.jpg/250px-Xi_Jinping_in_USA.jpg)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Xi_Jinping_in_USA.jpg)\n\nXi giving a speech at the U.S. Department of State in 2012, with then Secretary of State [Hillary Clinton](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hillary_Clinton \"Hillary Clinton\") and then Vice-president [Joe Biden](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joe_Biden \"Joe Biden\") in the background. Seated in the front row is former Secretary of State [Henry Kissinger](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_Kissinger \"Henry Kissinger\").\n\nXi has called China–United States relations in the contemporary world a \"new type of great-power relations\", a phrase the [Obama administration](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Presidency_of_Barack_Obama \"Presidency of Barack Obama\") had been reluctant to embrace.[[31]](#cite_note-nikkei2-31) According to Xi, the new type of great power relations is based on principles of \"no confrontati\n\n<error>Content truncated. Call the fetch tool with a start_index of 20000 to get more content.</error>"}],"structured_content":null},"error":null,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_38","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"fetch","tool":"fetch","arguments":{"max_length":10000,"url":"https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2024/country-chapters/china"},"result":null,"error":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_38","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"fetch","tool":"fetch","arguments":{"max_length":10000,"url":"https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2024/country-chapters/china"},"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Contents of https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2024/country-chapters/china:\nIn late 2022, the Chinese government abruptly ended its draconian “zero Covid” policy. As much of the population—and the medical establishment—was unprepared, Covid-19 infections, hospitalizations, and deaths surged. Officially, the government estimated that there were 60,000 “excess deaths” in December 2022; a[US academic study](https://www.reuters.com/world/china/nearly-2-million-excess-deaths-followed-chinas-sudden-end-covid-curbs-study-2023-08-25/) put the figure at 1.87 million deaths between December 2022 and January 2023. The true toll will likely remain unknown due to severe censorship.\n\nTen years into President Xi Jinping’s rule, repression deepens across the country. The Chinese government continues its abusive policies against Uyghurs and other Turkic Muslims in Xinjiang, which amount to crimes against humanity. In both Tibet and Xinjiang, those who contact family and friends abroad, or who advocate for their culture, language, and religion, risk being treated as “separatists” and have been given harsh prison sentences. Across China, the government is further tightening social controls. There is no independent civil society; even small pockets of freedom are eliminated. In Hong Kong, the Chinese government has assumed full control over the city since imposing the National Security Law in 2020.\n\nThe Chinese government’s list of political targets continues to expand. Even those who work for foreign entities in China’s cosmopolitan cities find themselves in the crosshairs, with revisions to a law on “counter-espionage” in July and with the state security ministry encouraging the population to report alleged spies.\n\nAs the economy slows, small protests have flared, such as those by pensioners against cuts to their medical insurance in February. Aspirations for a fairer society continue to run deep among many, especially those who have faced injustice, including women; lesbian, gay, bisexual, and transgender (LGBT) people; Tibetans; Uyghurs; Hong Kongers; and those at the bottom of the socioeconomic hierarchy. Some who went into exile have coalesced into diaspora activist movements.\n\nIn November, crowds of people across the country mourned the unexpected death of former Premier Li Keqiang. For many, Li represented a more economically vibrant China, in contrast to Xi, who prioritized social control and is taking China backward.\n\n## Freedom of Expression\n\nThrough laws and regulations, criminal punishment, harassment, intimidation, and the use of technology, the Chinese government operates one of the world’s most stringent censorship regimes.\n\nIn April, authorities [released Wuhan businessman Fang Bin](https://www.cnn.com/2023/05/04/china/fang-bin-wuhan-covid-19-citizen-journalist-released-intl-hnk/index.html), who was among the first to report on the Covid-19 outbreak, after three years in detention. But Shanghai-based activist and former lawyer Zhang Zhan, who went to Wuhan to report on the outbreak in February 2020, remains in custody. In July 2023, authorities transferred her to a prison hospital due to her deteriorating health.\n\nAuthorities released on bail some protesters who took part in the White Paper protests in late 2022, including Cao Zhixin, Li Yuanjing, Zhai Dengrui, and Li Siqi. But [in March](https://www.asahi.com/ajw/articles/14890817), a court sentenced Uyghur college student [Kamile Wayit](https://www.amnesty.org/en/petition/ask-chinese-authorities-to-release-19-year-old-uyghur-university-student-kamile-wayit/) to three years in prison for “promoting extremism” after she shared a video of the protests online. It is unknown how many protesters remain in detention.\n\nIn April, Chinese state security arrested a Taiwanese pro-independence politician [Yang Chih-yuan](https://hongkongfp.com/2023/04/26/china-charges-taiwanese-activist-yang-chih-yuan-with-secession/), who was living in Zhejiang province, on suspicion of “secession.”\n\nIn May, the Chinese government [fined](https://www.reuters.com/business/media-telecom/chinese-slaps-comedy-firm-with-2-mln-fine-after-military-joke-2023-05-17/) a comedy company 14.7 million renminbi (RMB) (about US$2 million) for a joke by stand-up comic Li Haoshi, who compared the People’s Liberation Army to dogs. In the same month, Weibo suspended a Malaysian comedian known as [Uncle Roger](https://apnews.com/article/china-uncle-roger-malaysia-comedian-censor-f79ce7de00787a122b0e2f162ff1e514) for joking about the Chinese government’s surveillance and sovereignty claims over Taiwan.\n\nIn August, a court in Guizhou province sentenced former economics professor, Yang Shaozheng, to 4.5 years in prison for “[inciting subversion](https://www.rfa.org/english/news/china/china-econ-prof-08312023153953.html)” after he raised concerns about the high annual cost of government personnel in China.\n\nIn September, the Chinese government released a draft regulation that, if passed, would penalize those who wear clothes in public that “hurt the Chinese people’s feelings.” The regulations appear to target those who wear Japanese clothing or items that challenge gender norms.\n\nThe Chinese government continues to rely on tech companies to enforce censorship. After a roof in Heilongjiang province collapsed, killing 10 children and the coach of a middle school volleyball team, authorities swiftly [censored the outpouring of grief](https://www.nytimes.com/2023/08/14/business/china-flooding-tragedy-mourning.html), including the names of the children.\n\nIn April, [researchers found](https://citizenlab.ca/2023/04/a-comparison-of-search-censorship-in-china/) that China’s search platforms use [60,000 rules](https://www.nytimes.com/2023/04/26/business/china-censored-search-engine.html) to censor online content; the most far-reaching political censorship among web search engines was by Microsoft’s Bing, its rules less numerous but broader and affecting more search results than the rules applied by Chinese companies such as Baidu. Bing said it would “look into the findings.” [In August](https://appleinsider.com/articles/23/08/02/apple-removes-over-100-chatgpt-like-apps-from-chinas-app-store), Apple reportedly removed over 100 generative Artificial Intelligence (AI) apps from China’s app store as Beijing cracked down on the new technology, in part to maintain social control. Apple said these apps “include content that is illegal in China.” In May, LinkedIn announced that it would phase out its app for China, [InCareer](https://edition.cnn.com/2023/05/09/tech/linkedin-layoffs-exit-china-app-intl-hnk/index.html), becoming the latest major US social media platform to shutter in China.\n\nIn May, the *Wall Street Journal* revealed that employees at TikTok, owned by Chinese company ByteDance, reportedly [monitored](https://www.businessinsider.com/tiktok-tracked-users-who-viewed-lgbtq-posts-report-2023-5?r=US&IR=T) content accessed by users, including those who watched LGBT content. TikTok said they had terminated such practices and that “safeguarding the privacy and security” of users is one of their “top priorities.” This is after a December 2022 revelation that the company [tracked](https://www.forbes.com/sites/emilybaker-white/2022/12/22/tiktok-tracks-forbes-journalists-bytedance/?sh=1884b53d7da5) American journalists who were writing critically about TikTok, which the company attributed to “the misconduct of certain individuals” who were no longer its employees.\n\n## Freedom of Religion\n\nState control over religion has grown since 2016, when President Xi called for “Sinicization” of religions. Police arrest, detain, and harass leaders and members of various “illegal” religious groups, including “house churches” (congregations that refuse to join official Catholic and Protestant churches). Authorities also disrupt their peaceful activities—whether they are preaching religion or running summer camps for children—and ban these groups outright.\n\nBeyond dictating what constitutes legal religious activity, authorities now seek to comprehensively reshape religions to be consistent with party ideology and promote allegiance to the party and Xi.\n\nIn provinces with a high proportion of Muslims, authorities have [closed down, demolished, or removed](https://www.hrw.org/news/2023/11/22/china-mosques-shuttered-razed-altered-muslim-areas) the minarets and domes of mosques to “Sinicize” them or to remove foreign influences. In Yunnan province, authorities removed Islamic features from Najiaying Mosque leading to a [rare protest](https://www.washingtonpost.com/world/2023/05/29/china-yunnan-mosque-hui-muslims/) by hundreds of Hui Muslims in May.\n\nThe Measures on the Administration of Religious Activity Venues came into force in September. They impose further restrictions on religious activities, requiring religious venues to indoctrinate followers in Chinese Communist Party ideology, and prohibiting them from “creating conflicts … between sects” or receiving funding other than those approved by the state.\n\nIn July, the Chinese government transferred a Catholic bishop, [Joseph Shen Bin](https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2023/7/15/pope-approves-shanghai-bishop-appointment-after-the-fact), to head the vacant Shanghai diocese, without consulting with the Vatican, in violation of the 2018 China-Vatican accord. While the Vatican publicly protested Beijing’s decision, it approved the move and has remained largely silent over Beijing’s religious persecution.\n\n## Attacks on Human Rights Defenders\n\nHuman rights defenders and government critics continue to face persecution.\n\nIn January, authorities released human rights lawyer Tang Jitian after forcibly disappearing him for 398 days. In March, a Guangxi court sentenced human rights lawyer Qin Yongpei to five years in prison for “inciting subversion of state power.” In April, a court in Shandong province sentenced prominent legal scholar Xu Zhiyong and human rights lawyer Ding Jiaxi to [14 and 12 years in prison](https://www.hrw.org/news/2023/04/10/china-quash-convictions-prominent-r\n\n<error>Content truncated. Call the fetch tool with a start_index of 10000 to get more content.</error>"}],"structured_content":null},"error":null,"status":"completed"}}
