{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_46","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"fetch","tool":"fetch","arguments":{"max_length":10000,"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China%E2%80%93United_States_relations#2023"},"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Contents of https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China%E2%80%93United_States_relations#2023:\nChina–United States relations\n\n|  |  |\n| --- | --- |\n| [Map indicating locations of China and United States](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:China_USA_Locator.svg \"Map indicating locations of China and United States\") | |\n|  | |\n|  |  |\n| [China](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China \"China\") | [United States](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States \"United States\") |\n| Diplomatic mission | |\n| [Chinese Embassy, Washington, D.C.](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Embassy_of_China,_Washington,_D.C. \"Embassy of China, Washington, D.C.\") | [United States Embassy, Beijing](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Embassy_of_the_United_States,_Beijing \"Embassy of the United States, Beijing\") |\n| Envoy | |\n| Ambassador [Xie Feng](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xie_Feng_(diplomat) \"Xie Feng (diplomat)\") | Ambassador [David Perdue](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_Perdue \"David Perdue\") |\n\n[![Donald Trump and Xi Jinping stand next to each other, both wearing suits](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Donald_Trump_visit_to_China%2C_May_2026_P20260514DT-1642.jpg/250px-Donald_Trump_visit_to_China%2C_May_2026_P20260514DT-1642.jpg)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Donald_Trump_visit_to_China,_May_2026_P20260514DT-1642.jpg)\n\nPresident [Donald Trump](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump \"Donald Trump\") with Chinese leader [Xi Jinping](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xi_Jinping \"Xi Jinping\") during his [state visit to China in 2026](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_state_visit_by_Donald_Trump_to_China \"2026 state visit by Donald Trump to China\")\n\nThe relationship between the [People's Republic of China](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/People's_Republic_of_China \"People's Republic of China\") (PRC) and the [United States](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States \"United States\") (US) has been complex and at times tense since the [establishment of the PRC](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proclamation_of_the_People's_Republic_of_China \"Proclamation of the People's Republic of China\") on 1 October 1949 and subsequent [retreat of the government of the Republic of China to Taiwan](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kuomintang's_retreat_to_Taiwan \"Kuomintang's retreat to Taiwan\"). After the normalization of relations in the 1970s, the US–China relationship has been marked by persistent disputes including China's economic policies, the [political status of Taiwan](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Political_status_of_Taiwan \"Political status of Taiwan\") and [territorial disputes in the South China Sea](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Territorial_disputes_in_the_South_China_Sea \"Territorial disputes in the South China Sea\"). Despite these tensions, the two nations have significant economic ties and are deeply interconnected, while also engaging in [strategic competition](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strategic_competition \"Strategic competition\") on the global stage. As of 2025, the US and China are the world's [largest and second-largest economies by nominal GDP](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_countries_by_GDP_(nominal) \"List of countries by GDP (nominal)\"). Collectively, they account for 44.2% of the global nominal GDP.\n\nThe proclamation of the PRC in Beijing by the [Chinese Communist Party](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_Communist_Party \"Chinese Communist Party\") (CCP) chairman [Mao Zedong](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mao_Zedong \"Mao Zedong\") created a new [central government](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Central_People's_Government_of_the_People's_Republic_of_China_(1949%E2%80%931954) \"Central People's Government of the People's Republic of China (1949–1954)\") on the [mainland](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mainland_China \"Mainland China\") in October 1949. Relations between the US and the new Chinese government quickly soured, culminating in direct conflict during the [Korean War](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Korean_War \"Korean War\"). The US-led [United Nations intervention](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Nations_Command \"United Nations Command\") was met with [Chinese military involvement](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China_in_the_Korean_War \"China in the Korean War\"), as China sent millions of soldiers to prevent a US-aligned presence on its border. For decades, the US did not formally recognize the PRC, instead maintaining diplomatic relations with the [Republic of China](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Republic_of_China \"Republic of China\") (ROC) based in Taiwan, and as such blocked the PRC's entry into the United Nations. However, shifting geopolitical dynamics, including the [Sino-Soviet split](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sino-Soviet_split \"Sino-Soviet split\"), the winding down of the [Vietnam War](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vietnam_War \"Vietnam War\"), as well as of the [Cultural Revolution](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cultural_Revolution \"Cultural Revolution\"), paved the way for US president [Richard Nixon's 1972 visit to China](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1972_visit_by_Richard_Nixon_to_China \"1972 visit by Richard Nixon to China\"), ultimately marking a sea change in US–China relations. On 1 January 1979, the US formally established diplomatic relations with the PRC and recognized it as the sole legitimate [government of China](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Government_of_China \"Government of China\"), while maintaining unofficial ties with Taiwan via the [Taiwan Relations Act](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taiwan_Relations_Act \"Taiwan Relations Act\"), which endures as a major point of contention.\n\nEvery US president since Nixon has toured China during his term in office, with the exception of [Jimmy Carter](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jimmy_Carter \"Jimmy Carter\") and [Joe Biden](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joe_Biden \"Joe Biden\"). The two countries cooperated with each other against the Soviet Union. Following China's [reform and opening up](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reform_and_opening_up \"Reform and opening up\"), the trade between the two countries significantly increased starting from the 1980s, and the US gave China the [most favored nation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Most_favoured_nation \"Most favoured nation\") designation in 2001. The [Obama administration](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Presidency_of_Barack_Obama \"Presidency of Barack Obama\") signed a record number of bilateral agreements with China, particularly regarding climate change, though its [Pivot to Asia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/East_Asian_foreign_policy_of_the_Obama_administration \"East Asian foreign policy of the Obama administration\") created diplomatic friction.\n\nRelations worsened during the 2010s over concerns including China's [militarization of the South China Sea](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Territorial_disputes_in_the_South_China_Sea#Chinese_policies \"Territorial disputes in the South China Sea\") and [Chinese espionage in the United States](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_espionage_in_the_United_States \"Chinese espionage in the United States\"),[[1]](#cite_note-bbc._WhyU-1)[[2]](#cite_note-2)[[3]](#cite_note-3) leading observers to speculate a [Second Cold War](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_Cold_War#Usage_in_the_context_of_China%E2%80%93United_States_tensions \"Second Cold War\") between the two powers.[[4]](#cite_note-4)[[5]](#cite_note-5) In 2018, President [Donald Trump](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump \"Donald Trump\") launched a [trade war with China](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China%E2%80%93United_States_trade_war \"China–United States trade war\"). The relationship further deteriorated during the [COVID-19 pandemic](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/COVID-19_pandemic \"COVID-19 pandemic\"). In January 2021, the US officially classified the Chinese government's treatment of the [Uyghurs](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uyghurs \"Uyghurs\") in Xinjiang [as a genocide](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Persecution_of_Uyghurs_in_China#Genocide \"Persecution of Uyghurs in China\").[[6]](#cite_note-Bernstein-2021-6) Tensions remained high during the [presidency of Joe Biden](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Presidency_of_Joe_Biden \"Presidency of Joe Biden\") from 2021. [His foreign policy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foreign_policy_of_the_Biden_administration#China \"Foreign policy of the Biden administration\") prioritized strategic competition with China,[[7]](#cite_note-7)[[8]](#cite_note-8) imposed [export controls on semiconductors to China](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_New_Export_Controls_on_Advanced_Computing_and_Semiconductors_to_China \"United States New Export Controls on Advanced Computing and Semiconductors to China\"),[[9]](#cite_note-9) boosted regional alliances against China, and expanded support for Taiwan.[[10]](#cite_note-Lau-2021-10)[[11]](#cite_note-11) However, the administration also emphasized that the US sought \"competition, not conflict\".[[12]](#cite_note-12) The [second Trump administration](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_presidency_of_Donald_Trump \"Second presidency of Donald Trump\")'s relations with China has been marked by inconsistency. From 2025, the administration sharply escalated the trade war with China, [raising tariffs](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tariffs_in_the_second_Trump_administration \"Tariffs in the second Trump administration\"), prior to negotiating with China on a reduction in the tariff rate. Trump administration has emphasized economic competition over ideological conflicts.\n\nThe [People's Republic of China](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China \"China\") (PRC) was founded on 1 October 1949.\n\nOn 25 June 1950, the China-aligned state of [North Korea](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_Korea \"North Korea\") invaded US-aligned [South Korea](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_Korea \"South Korea\").[[13]](#cite_note-Chen-1992-13) In response, the [United Nations Security Council](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Nations_Security_Council \"United Nations Security Council\") passed [Resolution 82](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Na\n\n<error>Content truncated. Call the fetch tool with a start_index of 10000 to get more content.</error>"}],"structured_content":null},"error":null,"status":"completed"}}
