{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_74","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"fetch","tool":"fetch","arguments":{"max_length":10000,"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China_and_weapons_of_mass_destruction"},"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Contents of https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China_and_weapons_of_mass_destruction:\n| [People's Republic of China](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China \"China\") | |\n| --- | --- |\n| [DF-61](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DF-61 \"DF-61\") [intercontinental ballistic missiles](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Intercontinental_ballistic_missiles \"Intercontinental ballistic missiles\") on display during the [2025 China Victory Day Parade](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025_China_Victory_Day_Parade \"2025 China Victory Day Parade\") | |\n| [Controlled territory of PRC](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:CHN_orthographic.svg \"Controlled territory of PRC\") People's Republic of China  [Territory claimed but not controlled](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Territorial_disputes_of_the_People's_Republic_of_China \"Territorial disputes of the People's Republic of China\") | |\n| Nuclear program start date | 1955 (71 years ago) |\n| First nuclear weapon test | October 16, 1964 |\n| First thermonuclear weapon test | December 28, 1966 |\n| Last nuclear test | July 29, 1996 |\n| Largest yield test | 4 Mt  * Atmospheric – **4 Mt** (November 17, 1976) * Underground – **660~1,000 kt** (May 21, 1992) |\n| Current stockpile | 620 (estimated)[[1]](#cite_note-FAS_World-1) |\n| Maximum missile range | 20,000 km[[2]](#cite_note-2) |\n| Nuclear triad | **Yes**[[3]](#cite_note-:284-3) |\n| [NPT](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nuclear_Non-Proliferation_Treaty \"Nuclear Non-Proliferation Treaty\") party | **Yes** (1992, one of five recognized [nuclear-weapon states](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_states_with_nuclear_weapons#Recognized_nuclear-weapon_states \"List of states with nuclear weapons\")) |\n\nThe [People's Republic of China](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/People's_Republic_of_China \"People's Republic of China\") has [possessed nuclear weapons](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nuclear_weapons_of_China \"Nuclear weapons of China\") since 1964. It was the last to develop them of the five [nuclear-weapon states](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nuclear-weapon_states \"Nuclear-weapon states\") recognized by the [Nuclear Non-Proliferation Treaty](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Treaty_on_the_Non-Proliferation_of_Nuclear_Weapons \"Treaty on the Non-Proliferation of Nuclear Weapons\") (NPT). China acceded to the [Biological Weapons Convention](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Biological_Weapons_Convention \"Biological Weapons Convention\") (BWC) in 1984,[[4]](#cite_note-4) acceded to the NPT in 1992,[[5]](#cite_note-5) and ratified the [Chemical Weapons Convention](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chemical_Weapons_Convention \"Chemical Weapons Convention\") (CWC) in 1997.[[6]](#cite_note-:6-6)\n\nChina [tested its first nuclear bomb](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Project_596 \"Project 596\") in 1964 and [its first full-scale thermonuclear bomb](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Project_639 \"Project 639\") in 1967. It carried out 45 [nuclear tests](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_nuclear_weapons_tests_of_China \"List of nuclear weapons tests of China\") before signing the [Comprehensive Nuclear-Test-Ban Treaty](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comprehensive_Nuclear-Test-Ban_Treaty \"Comprehensive Nuclear-Test-Ban Treaty\") in 1996.\n\nChina is estimated to possess a stockpile of approximately 620 nuclear warheads as of 2026,[[1]](#cite_note-FAS_World-1) making it the [third-largest](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_countries_with_nuclear_weapons#Estimated_worldwide_nuclear_stockpiles \"List of countries with nuclear weapons\") in the world. It is the only NPT nuclear-weapon state significantly expanding its arsenal and is projected to reach 1,000 warheads by 2030 and up to 1,500 by 2035. Compared to the [arsenals of the United States](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nuclear_weapons_of_the_United_States \"Nuclear weapons of the United States\") and [Russia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russia_and_weapons_of_mass_destruction#Nuclear_weapons \"Russia and weapons of mass destruction\"), a much smaller proportion of China's warheads are believed to be deployed on their [delivery systems](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nuclear_weapons_delivery \"Nuclear weapons delivery\"), with the remainder stored separately.[[7]](#cite_note-:32-7)\n\nSince 2020, the [People's Liberation Army](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/People's_Liberation_Army \"People's Liberation Army\") has operated a [nuclear triad](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nuclear_triad \"Nuclear triad\").[[3]](#cite_note-:284-3) Of its 600 warheads, it is estimated 376 are assigned to its [Rocket Force](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/People's_Liberation_Army_Rocket_Force \"People's Liberation Army Rocket Force\")'s [*Dongfeng*](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dongfeng_(missile) \"Dongfeng (missile)\") [intermediate](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Intermediate-range_ballistic_missile \"Intermediate-range ballistic missile\") and [intercontinental ballistic missiles](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Intercontinental_ballistic_missile \"Intercontinental ballistic missile\"), 72 to its [Navy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/People's_Liberation_Army_Navy \"People's Liberation Army Navy\")'s [*Julang*-3](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Julang_3 \"Julang 3\") [submarine-launched ballistic missiles](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Submarine-launched_ballistic_missile \"Submarine-launched ballistic missile\") on six [Type 094 submarines](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Type_094_submarine \"Type 094 submarine\"), and 20 to its [Air Force](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/People's_Liberation_Army_Air_Force \"People's Liberation Army Air Force\")'s [*Jinglei*-1](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/JL-1_(air-launched_ballistic_missile) \"JL-1 (air-launched ballistic missile)\") [air-launched ballistic missiles](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Air-launched_ballistic_missile \"Air-launched ballistic missile\") for 20 [Xi'an H-6N](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xi'an_H-6 \"Xi'an H-6\") [strategic bombers](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strategic_bomber \"Strategic bomber\"). Approximately 132 warheads await assignment. China is upgrading its triad with the in-development [Xi'an H-20](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xi'an_H-20 \"Xi'an H-20\") [stealth bomber](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stealth_aircraft \"Stealth aircraft\"), [Type 096 submarine](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Type_096_submarine \"Type 096 submarine\"), and large [missile silo](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Missile_silo \"Missile silo\") fields. China fields [multiple independently targetable reentry vehicles](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multiple_independently_targetable_reentry_vehicle \"Multiple independently targetable reentry vehicle\"), and has tested [hypersonic glide vehicles](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hypersonic_glide_vehicle \"Hypersonic glide vehicle\") and a [fractional orbital bombardment system](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fractional_orbital_bombardment_system \"Fractional orbital bombardment system\").[[8]](#cite_note-:384-8)\n\nIn 1964, China adopted a policy of [no-first-use](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/No_first_use \"No first use\") (NFU) and called for an international NFU treaty,[[9]](#cite_note-9)[[10]](#cite_note-:9-10) both of which it continues to advocate.[[11]](#cite_note-11)[[12]](#cite_note-t849-12) Some of its nuclear forces are reported to have moved toward a [launch on warning](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Launch_on_warning \"Launch on warning\") (LOW) posture in the early 2020s.[[13]](#cite_note-International_Security_2023-13)[[14]](#cite_note-:11-14)\n\nChina denies currently possessing offensive [chemical](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_chemical_weapons_program?action=edit&redlink=1 \"Chinese chemical weapons program (page does not exist)\") and [biological weapons programs](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_biological_weapons_program \"Chinese biological weapons program\"), while the US alleges it is not in compliance with treaty obligations.[[15]](#cite_note-:16-15) In its 1997 non-public declaration to the [OPCW](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Organisation_for_the_Prohibition_of_Chemical_Weapons \"Organisation for the Prohibition of Chemical Weapons\"), China reportedly declared two or three chemical weapon production facilities, now widely believed to be destroyed. Experts suggest this program focused on [mustard gas](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mustard_gas \"Mustard gas\") and [lewisite](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lewisite \"Lewisite\").[[16]](#cite_note-u249-16)[[17]](#cite_note-17)\n\nDuring the [Second Sino-Japanese War](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_Sino-Japanese_War \"Second Sino-Japanese War\"), the [Imperial Japanese Army](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Imperial_Japanese_Army \"Imperial Japanese Army\")'s [biological warfare department](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Epidemic_Prevention_and_Water_Purification_Department \"Epidemic Prevention and Water Purification Department\"), led by [Unit 731](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unit_731 \"Unit 731\"), dispersed [anthrax](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anthrax \"Anthrax\"), [cholera](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cholera \"Cholera\"), [dysentery](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dysentery \"Dysentery\"), [typhoid](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Typhoid_fever \"Typhoid fever\"), [plague](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plague_(disease) \"Plague (disease)\"), and other pathogens, killing between 200,000 and 500,000 people. Japanese forces also [used chemical weapons](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_chemical_warfare#Imperial_Japanese_Army \"History of chemical warfare\") including mustard gas and lewisite, causing an estimated 37,000 to 80,000 casualties.[[18]](#cite_note-18) Some 700,000 to 2 million Japanese chemical weapons were abandoned in China, with less than 100,000 recovered as of 2023.[[19]](#cite_note-o579-19)[[20]](#cite_note-u877-20)\n\n[Chinese Communist Party](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_Communist_Party \"Chinese Communist Party\") (CCP) [chairman](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chairman_of_the_Chinese_Communist_Party \"Chairman of the Chinese Communist Party\") [Mao Zedong](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mao_Zedong \"Mao Zedong\") referred to nuclear weapons as a [pape\n\n<error>Content truncated. Call the fetch tool with a start_index of 10000 to get more content.</error>"}],"structured_content":null},"error":null,"status":"completed"}}
