{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_82","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"fetch","tool":"fetch","arguments":{"max_length":8000,"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poverty_in_China"},"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Contents of https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poverty_in_China:\n|  |  |\n| --- | --- |\n|  | Parts of this article (those related to documentation) need to be **updated**. Please help update this article to reflect recent events or newly available information. *(May 2020)* |\n\n[![](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Poverty_in_China.svg/250px-Poverty_in_China.svg.png)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Poverty_in_China.svg)\n\nShare of population in extreme poverty over time\n\nThe majority of poverty in the [People's Republic of China](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/People's_Republic_of_China \"People's Republic of China\") (PRC) is [rural poverty](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rural_poverty \"Rural poverty\"). Decades of economic development has reduced urban [extreme poverty](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Extreme_poverty \"Extreme poverty\").[[1]](#cite_note-:1-1)[[2]](#cite_note-2)[[3]](#cite_note-3) According to the [World Bank](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_Bank \"World Bank\"), more than 850 million Chinese people have been lifted out of extreme poverty; China's poverty rate fell from 88 percent in 1981 to 0.7 percent in 2015, as measured by the percentage of people living on the equivalent of US$1.90 or less per day in 2011 purchasing price parity terms,[[4]](#cite_note-worldbank.org-4)[[5]](#cite_note-5) which still stands in 2022.[[6]](#cite_note-WBPL-6)[[7]](#cite_note-PLWB-7)[[8]](#cite_note-ChinaEradicateAbsolutePoverty-8)\n\nThe Chinese definition of extreme poverty is more stringent than that of the World Bank: earning less than $2.30 a day at [purchasing power parity](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Purchasing_power_parity \"Purchasing power parity\") (PPP).[[9]](#cite_note-ChinaUmicPoverty-9) Growth has fueled a substantial increase in per-capita income, lifting people out of extreme poverty. China's per capita income saw a five-fold increase, from $200 to $1,000 between 1990 and 2000. The same rate of increase was observed from 2000 to 2010, bringing the per capita income up to $5,000. This moved China into the ranks of middle-income countries.[[1]](#cite_note-:1-1)\n\n[Kent Deng](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kent_Deng \"Kent Deng\") writes in 2000 that by 1978, two thirds of China's rural population had a living standard lower than that of 1958, and under Mao, China became impoverished to a greater extent than India and USSR. Almost half of households lived in poverty in 1978, two years after [Mao Zedong](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mao_Zedong \"Mao Zedong\")'s death. Unlike in [Khrushchev](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nikita_Khrushchev \"Nikita Khrushchev\")'s USSR or even [Kim Il Sung](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kim_Il_Sung \"Kim Il Sung\")'s North Korea, under Mao, poverty reduction was not given attention to, and the government instead engaged in \"campaigns of class struggle\" to distract the people, as poverty perpetuated itself.[[10]](#cite_note-KDeng-10) Nevertheless, under Mao, \"China’s government provided food and shelter at little or no cost. This meant US$1.90 was able to buy more basic necessities in China than in comparable capitalist countries.\"[[11]](#cite_note-11)[[12]](#cite_note-12) Prior to the 1978 market reforms, poverty in China was already considered less severe than in over developing nations. According to one journal in 1981, “the poorest of China’s poor are better fed, better educated and healthier than the poorest in India and Pakistan, which makes these Chinese less poor.” Life expectancy in China, estimated by the World Bank at 64, was 3 years higher than the average for middle-income countries, while studies found that even before 1978 there was less malnutrition in China than other poor nations, while also having higher rates of literacy and school enrolment and a far lower rate of infant mortality.[[13]](#cite_note-13) According to one measurement, the percentage of people in rural areas living in poverty fell from 30.7% in 1978 to 3.5% in 2000.[[14]](#cite_note-14)\n\nBetween 1990 and 2005, China's progress accounted for more than three-quarters of global poverty reduction and was largely responsible for the world reaching the UN millennium development target of halving extreme poverty. This can be attributed to a combination of a rapidly expanding labor market, driven by a protracted period of economic growth, and a series of government transfers, such as an urban subsidy, and the introduction of a rural pension.[[15]](#cite_note-15) The World Bank Group said that the percentage of the population living below the international poverty line of $1.9 (2011 PPP) fell to 0.7 percent in 2015, and the poverty line of $3.2 (2011 PPP) fell to 7% in 2015.[[4]](#cite_note-worldbank.org-4)\n\nAt the end of 2018, the number of people living below China's national poverty line of [¥](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Renminbi \"Renminbi\")2,300 ([CNY](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Renminbi \"Renminbi\")) per year (in 2010 constant prices) was 16.6 million, equal to 1.7% of the population at the time. On November 23, 2020, China announced that it had eliminated absolute poverty nationwide by uplifting all of its citizens beyond its set ¥2,300 per year (in 2010 constant prices),[[16]](#cite_note-16) or around ¥4,000 per year in 2020.[[17]](#cite_note-17) The World Bank has different [poverty lines](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poverty_line \"Poverty line\") for countries with a different [gross national income](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gross_national_income \"Gross national income\") (GNI). With a GNI per capita of $10,610 in 2020,[[18]](#cite_note-ChinaGNIperCapita-18) China is an upper middle-income country.[[19]](#cite_note-ChinaUmic-19)[[20]](#cite_note-CountriesByGNIperCapita-20) The poverty line for an upper middle-income country is $5.5 per day at PPP.[[6]](#cite_note-WBPL-6)[[7]](#cite_note-PLWB-7)[[8]](#cite_note-ChinaEradicateAbsolutePoverty-8)[[9]](#cite_note-ChinaUmicPoverty-9) As of 2020, China has succeeded in eradicating [absolute poverty](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Absolute_poverty \"Absolute poverty\"),[[8]](#cite_note-ChinaEradicateAbsolutePoverty-8)[[9]](#cite_note-ChinaUmicPoverty-9)[[21]](#cite_note-ChinEradicateExtremePoverty-21) but not the poverty defined for upper middle-income countries which China belongs to.[[22]](#cite_note-ChinaNoLicPoverty-22)[[21]](#cite_note-ChinEradicateExtremePoverty-21) China still has around 13% of its population falling below this poverty line of $5.50 per day in 2020.[[9]](#cite_note-ChinaUmicPoverty-9) In 2020, premier [Li Keqiang](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Li_Keqiang \"Li Keqiang\"), citing the [National Bureau of Statistics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Bureau_of_Statistics_of_China \"National Bureau of Statistics of China\") (NBS) said that China still had 600 million people living with less than 1000 yuan ($140) a month, although an article from *[The Economist](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Economist \"The Economist\")* said that the methodology NBS used was flawed, stating that the figure took the combined income, which was then equally divided.[[23]](#cite_note-23)\n\nSince [Deng Xiaoping](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deng_Xiaoping \"Deng Xiaoping\") began instituting market reforms in the late 1970s, China has been among the most rapidly growing economies in the world, regularly exceeding 10 percent GDP growth annually from 1978 through 2010.[[24]](#cite_note-24) This growth has led to a substantial increase in real [living standards](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Standard_of_living \"Standard of living\") and a marked decline in [poverty](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poverty \"Poverty\"). Between 1981 and 2008, the proportion of China's population living on less than $1.25/day is estimated to have fallen from 85% to 13.1%, meaning that roughly 600 million people were taken out of extreme poverty.[[25]](#cite_note-Anup-25)[[26]](#cite_note-26) At the same time, this rapid change has brought with it different kinds of stresses. China faces serious [natural resource](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Natural_resource \"Nat\n\n<error>Content truncated. Call the fetch tool with a start_index of 8000 to get more content.</error>"}],"structured_content":null},"error":null,"status":"completed"}}
