{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_85","type":"agent_message","text":"The sandbox is completely restricted - no commands can run and no files can be written. Per the contract: \"If writing either is unavailable, continue the research and put the complete handoff in the final response for parent persistence.\" I'll deliver the full evidence dossier below.\n\n---\n\n# Research Handoff — Xi Jinping (China, 2023)\n\n## Executive Summary\n\nThis dossier covers Xi Jinping's ruler-period for calendar year 2023. Local-prior structured evidence (36 unique facts) was supplied by the parent; internet/manual evidence was collected via direct fetches because the primary web-search backend intermittently failed. Per the compact execution contract, no scores, score ranges, or anchor rankings are produced.\n\n**Coverage summary:**\n- Chapters with local-prior coverage: 2B, 3B, 4B, 5B, 7B, 8B\n- Chapters requiring narrative research (no local prior): 1B (nuclear), 6B (social welfare)\n- All 80 lens IDs across 8 chapters (1B.1–1B.10, 2B.1–2B.10, 3B.1–3B.10, 4B.1–4B.10, 5B.1–5B.10, 6B.1–6B.10, 7B.1–7B.10, 8B.1–8B.10) covered with at least some supporting evidence; 6B and 1B rely more heavily on narrative material because the local-prior set was empty for those scopes.\n\n## Chapter 1B — Nuclear and Existential-Risk Responsibility\n\n**Local prior:** Empty for this chapter (D17/FAS nuclear facts absent from package); supplied rationale notes \"absence of a row is not evidence of responsible ruler conduct.\"\n\n**Ruler exposure profile:** High material exposure. China is one of five NPT nuclear-weapon states, is the only NPT nuclear-weapon state significantly expanding its arsenal, and the U.S. DoD projects 1,500 warheads by 2035 (CSIS, Wikipedia, FAS estimates). Xi is CCP General Secretary, President, and Chairman of the Central Military Commission — full authority over doctrine, forces, appointments, and crisis decisions.\n\n**Reusable evidence items (chapter 1B):**\n\n- **E1B-01** — *Provisional ID.* **Canonical key:** en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China_and_weapons_of_mass_destruction::FAS_World-stockpile_2026. **Locator:** Wikipedia article \"China and weapons of mass destruction,\" infobox \"Current stockpile: 620 (estimated)\" citing FAS World, accessed 2026; effective for 2023 trend: stockpile was already growing rapidly. **Source type/confidence:** high (encyclopedia aggregating FAS Bulletin of Atomic Scientists data). **Claim:** \"China is the only NPT nuclear-weapon state significantly expanding its arsenal and is projected to reach 1,000 warheads by 2030 and up to 1,500 by 2035.\" **Period fit:** target-year trend; describes continuing expansion Xi oversaw in 2023. **Attribution:** Xi holds command authority over nuclear forces (CMC Chairman). **Role:** contextual negative (expansion). **Lenses:** 1B.4 (arms-control vs. expansion), 1B.10 (end posture). **Note:** Wikipedia date stamp is 2026 but the claim describes the trajectory through 2023; FAS Nuclear Notebook is the underlying primary source (paywalled direct PDF, retrieved only via aggregator).\n- **E1B-02** — **Canonical key:** csis.org/chinas-military-10-charts::china-growing-nuclear-warhead-stockpile. **Locator:** CSIS \"China's Military in 10 Charts,\" section \"6. China's Growing Nuclear Warhead Stockpile.\" **Source type/confidence:** medium_high (think tank, citing Bulletin/FAS). **Claim:** \"China's nuclear stockpile reached 600 warheads in 2025, more than doubling since 2019. The DOD forecasts that China will continue to expand its nuclear arsenal to reach some 1,500 warheads by 2035.\" **Period fit:** describes 2019–2025 trajectory including 2023. **Role:** contextual negative (significant expansion). **Lenses:** 1B.4, 1B.10.\n- **E1B-03** — **Canonical key:** csis.org/chinas-military-10-charts::xi-taiwan-2027-deadline. **Locator:** CSIS \"China's Military in 10 Charts,\" section \"5. The Cross-Strait Power Imbalance.\" **Source type/confidence:** medium_high. **Claim:** \"Xi Jinping has ordered the military to be able to seize Taiwan by force by 2027.\" **Period fit:** target-period policy stated in 2023. **Role:** contrary/contextual — raises dual-use nuclear risk against regional target. **Lenses:** 1B.5 (using nuclear capability for coercion).\n- **E1B-04** — **Canonical key:** en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China_and_weapons_of_mass_destruction::no-first-use-policy. **Locator:** Wikipedia article \"China and weapons of mass destruction,\" section on NFU policy. **Source type/confidence:** medium_high (encyclopedia). **Claim:** \"In 1964, China adopted a policy of no-first-use (NFU) and called for an international NFU treaty, both of which it continues to advocate. Some of its nuclear forces are reported to have moved toward a launch on warning (LOW) posture in the early 2020s.\" **Period fit:** policy continuity plus reported LOW shift in early 2020s. **Role:** mixed — formal NFU maintained; LOW shift raises risk. **Lenses:** 1B.1, 1B.3, 1B.5.\n- **E1B-05** — **Canonical key:** en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2023_in_China::central-military-commission-purges-late-2023. **Locator:** Wikipedia \"2023 in China,\" entry 24 October 2023. **Source type/confidence:** medium_high (encyclopedia citing primary news). **Claim:** \"24 October — The Standing Committee of the National People's Congress of China votes to remove several State Council members from their positions, including defense minister Li Shangfu, finance minister Liu Kun, and science minister Wang Zhigang.\" **Period fit:** 2023 specific event. **Role:** contextual — defense-minister purge during continued nuclear buildup; signals command-discipline interventions but also personnel churn. **Lenses:** 1B.3 (command/control discipline), 1B.7 (expert vs. loyalty appointments).\n\n**1B chapter assessment note for formatter:** Material exposure; expansion of stockpile; alleged 2027 Taiwan contingency; LOW posture shift; defense-minister purge. No documented 2023 arms-control breakthrough; CTBT