l baseline; reflects earlier policy legacy. **Lenses:** 6B.3, 6B.7, 6B.10.\n- **E6B-07** — **Canonical key:** china-briefing.com_news_chinas-gdp-in-2023::cpi-0.2-percent. **Locator:** China Briefing same article. **Claim:** \"CPI: +0.2%\" for 2023 — very low inflation. **Period fit:** 2023. **Role:** contextual for living standards. **Lenses:** 6B.9.\n- **E6B-08** — **Canonical key:** china-briefing.com_news_chinas-gdp-in-2023::unemployment-5.2. **Locator:** China Briefing same article. **Claim:** \"Unemployment rate: 5.2%.\" **Period fit:** 2023. **Role:** contextual for welfare/labor. **Lenses:** 6B.9.\n- **E6B-09** — **Canonical key:** hrw.org/world-report/2024/country-chapters/china::women-law-amendments. **Locator:** HRW World Report 2024, \"Women's and Girls' Rights.\" **Claim:** \"On January 1, revisions to the 1992 Law on the Protection of Women's Rights and Interests came into effect, which addresses workplace discrimination and sexual harassment.\" **Period fit:** 1 January 2023. **Role:** positive (limited) for 6B.8; but HRW notes the amendments also tell women to \"respect social morals … and family values\" — mixed. **Lenses:** 6B.7, 6B.8.\n\n**6B chapter assessment note:** Sparse evidence with significant negative attribution for COVID-19 excess deaths following the abrupt zero-COVID reversal. Demographic decline is documented (deaths > births). Limited positive: 1 January 2023 women's rights law revision; local cash rewards for births. Significant evidence gaps for health/education/welfare service delivery quality in 2023 — primary national statistics not retrieved.\n\n**Lens coverage for 6B:** All ten lenses have at least partial evidence; 6B.2, 6B.4, 6B.5 have weakest coverage.\n\n**Source diversity for 6B:** 3 source families (HRW, Wikipedia/Reuters, China Briefing/NBS).\n\n**Notable gap:** No direct WHO/UNICEF/UNDP 2023 China-specific HDI/health/education program data retrieved. The dossier above is the available evidence; further depth would require additional research time.\n\n## Chapter 7B — Ruler Personal Integrity and Honesty\n\n**Local prior:** LF012 (accountability −1.275), LF028 (rule of law 0.232), LF033 (corruption index 0.525), LF034 (CPI score 42/100), LF035 (executive corruption 0.534), LF036 (public corruption 0.498). All ten 7B lenses have candidate mappings.\n\n**Reusable evidence items (chapter 7B):**\n\n- **E7B-01 (local-prior set)** — LF033 (V-Dem political corruption index 0.525, midpoint), LF034 (Transparency CPI 42/100, well below midpoint), LF035 (V-Dem executive corruption 0.534), LF036 (V-Dem public corruption 0.498). **Role:** structural baseline; not necessarily ruler-specific. **Lenses:** 7B.3, 7B.4, 7B.5, 7B.9.\n- **E7B-02 (local-prior)** — LF012, LF028 (rule of law). **Lenses:** 7B.4, 7B.7, 7B.10.\n- **E7B-03** — **Canonical key:** en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anti-corruption_campaign_under_Xi_Jinping::2.3-million-officials-as-of-2023. **Locator:** Wikipedia \"Anti-corruption campaign under Xi Jinping.\" **Source type/confidence:** medium_high. **Claim:** \"As of 2023, approximately 2.3 million government officials had been prosecuted.\" **Period fit:** through 2023. **Role:** decisive positive for 7B.5 (purported professional anti-corruption effort); **Lenses:** 7B.5, 7B.6, 7B.10.\n- **E7B-04** — **Canonical key:** en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anti-corruption_campaign_under_Xi_Jinping::ccdi-centralization. **Locator:** Wikipedia article, lead section. **Claim:** \"Upon assuming office, Xi pledged to crack down on both 'tigers and flies'… Most of the officials investigated were dismissed from office and faced charges of bribery and abuse of power… Xi, who also directs anti-graft efforts of the military through his holding the office of Chairman of the Central Military Commission (i.e., commander-in-chief). The majority of reporting on media sources has highlighted Xi Jinping's direct involvement in managing the campaign.\" **Period fit:** ongoing 2023. **Role:** positive for 7B.5, 7B.6; mixed